// Unit tests for the text field's state machine -- the buffer, the caret, the
// key transitions and the synthesised key repeat.
//
// It is driven the way tests/unit/sys drives the key state: inp_stamp with the
// codes the backend really sends, and the frame ordinal bumped by hand at each
// frame boundary. No window, no display, no clock -- the time is a parameter, so
// the repeat timings are exact rather than approximately sixty times a second.
//
// The expected values were worked out from the transition table and the two
// durations, by hand, before the code was run: 420 ms before the first repeat
// and 30 ms between them, so a key pressed at 5000 fires at 5000, again at the
// first frame at or after 5420, and again at the first frame at or after 30 ms
// past that. The frames below straddle each of those deadlines on both sides,
// which is the only way to tell a repeat that works from one that fires every
// frame.
//
// txt_init first: the printable range tin_key tests against is the loaded face's
// ((import txt_first)/txt_last()), and those read an exported global.

main(int argc, string[] argv) {
  txt_init();
  inp_init();
  t_boot();
} return int 0;
